Author the manifest.json entry for a new example site named "__NAME__" in this
repository (hwaro-examples). This is the art-direction step — no site code is
written here. The entry you produce is the complete assignment the builder
model will execute, so every field must be decided and precise.

Everything that makes a site memorable is decided HERE. The builder executes;
it does not rescue a safe assignment. A brief that could describe any site in
its category produces a site that looks like any site in its category.

Read first: DESIGN.md §1-2, §13 (both the tells table and "Where the upstream
skill and this collection differ") and §15 steps 1-2, then AGENTS.md "Tag
vocabulary", then the hwaro-design skill appended at the end of this prompt.
DESIGN.md governs this collection's own constraints (the diversity matrix,
manifest shape, process); the skill is the upstream, actively-maintained source
for taste calls DESIGN.md doesn't spell out — the Design Read (§0), the three
dials (§1), palette and typography discipline (§4), the pattern vocabulary
(§6), the AI tells (§9). Where the two conflict on a collection-specific rule
(font-pairing cap, hue-distance floor, em-dash usage in prose), DESIGN.md wins.

Process:
1. Confirm the name "__NAME__" is legal: not in manifest.json, not in
   scripts/retired-names.txt, not a reserved word (see DESIGN.md §15.1),
   matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9]*$. If it is illegal, STOP and report why instead
   of inventing a different name.
2. Analyze the collection for gaps before choosing anything:
   - category distribution and scheme balance (manifest.json)
   - font pairings at the 5-use cap (count every display/text pair; choose
     one from the DESIGN.md §5 pool with 4 or fewer uses)
   - accent hues already used in your chosen category+scheme bucket (keep
     >= 10 degrees of hue distance)
   - layouts and signatures in the chosen category (your signature must be
     unique collection-wide; prefer under-used layouts)
3. STUDY THE FIELD. Read the full manifest entries — brief, layout, signature,
   palette — of the three examples nearest to yours (same category, same
   scheme first). Write one line per peer describing what its first screen
   looks like. This is the set your site will be mistaken for if you play it
   safe, and the reviewer will judge "__NAME__" against these same peers.
4. DECLARE THE DESIGN READ (skill §0.B), one line:
   "Reading this as: <site kind> for <audience>, with a <vibe> language,
   leaning toward <aesthetic family>. Dials: <variance>/<motion>/<density>."
   Reason the dial values from the category and styles using skill §1.A —
   never assume a baseline. Docs and books sit low on variance and motion;
   landings, portfolios and galleries sit high. These three numbers go into
   the entry as `dials` and become a contract: the builder must ship them and
   the reviewer checks them against the rendered page.
5. PITCH THREE CONCEPTS, KILL TWO. Write three genuinely different directions
   for this site — different signature, different first-screen composition,
   different emotional register. Not three palettes of one idea. For each,
   state in two lines: the signature move, and what the 1280x720 first screen
   looks like. Then judge them:
   - Which is closest to what a generic <category> site looks like? Kill it.
   - Which one, at thumbnail size, could NOT be confused with any peer from
     step 3? That is usually the winner.
   - Which one can a builder actually execute in plain CSS on a static site
     with no binary assets? A concept that needs photography is dead here.
   Commit to one and say in one line why it beat the other two. Carry the
   losers' best single detail into the winner if it fits.
6. THE SIGNATURE MUST BE SCENE-LEVEL. A scene-level signature changes how the
   whole first screen reads — a skyline the content sits inside, a broadsheet
   rule system the entire page obeys, a terminal frame that owns the viewport,
   an oversized specimen the layout bends around. A detail-level signature
   decorates a corner — a monogram, a badge, one styled date, an accent
   underline. Detail-level signatures are why sites get demoted from the
   gallery front. State the signature concretely enough to implement in CSS,
   and state which band of the first screen carries it.
7. Write the complete entry, matching the exact JSON shape of existing
   entries: name, title, description (20-140 chars, gallery-ready), category,
   scheme, styles (1-2), dials {variance, motion, density}, brief, typography
   {display, text, mono}, palette (+palette_dark for auto/dark sites), layout,
   signature, content {theme, sections, standalone}, features.
   - `brief` is a vivid, concrete paragraph the builder can execute without
     judgment. Name the bands of the homepage IN ORDER, and for each band say
     what real matter it carries (a listing, a specimen, an SVG diagram, a
     data rail — not "a hero section"). Give the first-screen bands an
     approximate pixel height and make them sum to under 720 so the signature
     lands above the fold. Name the voice the content is written in (a field
     researcher's log, a printer's colophon, a release engineer's changelog) —
     the builder writes the prose and needs the register.
   - The content plan must yield 6-10 markdown files.
8. Palette discipline: compute WCAG contrast for fg/bg, muted/bg, accent/bg,
   accent_fg/accent — all must be >= 4.5. State the computed ratios in your
   report. No pure #000/#fff in dark palettes; desaturate the dark accent.
   No AI-purple violet-to-blue gradient direction (DESIGN.md §13).
9. ANTI-DEFAULT CHECK before you insert. Answer these in the report:
   - Write one sentence describing the generic <category> site: the layout
     everyone reaches for. Then one sentence on how this entry diverges. If
     you cannot name a real divergence, go back to step 5.
   - Does the first screen carry real matter beyond a heading and a
     paragraph? A headline + subtitle + two pill buttons + one illustration
     is the single most common failure in this pipeline. If that describes
     your first screen, redesign it.
   - Run the skill §9 tells against the brief itself: is the signature a
     free-floating decoration? Is the accent budget blown across headings,
     borders and backgrounds? Is the header the default logo-left-links-right?
10. INSERT the entry into manifest.json in alphabetical order by name. This is
    strictly additive: never modify, rename, or delete any existing entry, and
    never reuse another entry's brief/palette/signature with the name swapped —
    a near-duplicate of an existing entry is a hard failure (§13 "no recolored
    clones"), even if the validator passes.
11. Run scripts/validate-manifest.py — it must exit 0 with no new warnings.
    Then run `scripts/agent/peers.sh __NAME__`: it prints the peers the design
    reviewer will judge this site against. If they are not the three you
    studied in step 3, study the ones it names and confirm your entry still
    diverges from them.

Report, in this order: the three peers from step 3, the Design Read with dial
reasoning, the three concepts and why the winner won, the anti-default check
from step 9, the inserted entry verbatim, the contrast ratios, the pairing use
count, the hue distances in the bucket, and the validator output.
Do NOT create examples/__NAME__/ and do NOT git commit — manifest entry only.

